<!--
  /styling — the <style> block, scoping, and the token convention.
  Component styles live inside their own .sui file (no separate stylesheet
  to coordinate) and resolve their tokens from the document root.
-->
<div class="doc styling-doc">
  <p class="eyebrow">Guides</p>
  <h1>Styling</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    SUI components style themselves inline. A <code>&lt;style&gt;</code>
    block in a <code>.sui</code> file ships with the component, scopes to
    the host element, and reads tokens from the document root. There is no
    global stylesheet to coordinate, no CSS-in-JS runtime, and no separate
    bundle to ship alongside the component.
  </p>

  <h2>Inline <code>&lt;style&gt;</code></h2>
  <p>
    Place a <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> block anywhere in the template. The
    compiler hoists it into the component's shadow root at build time.
    The rules apply to the host element and its descendants; nothing
    outside the shadow root is affected.
  </p>
  <sui-codeblock code={code.basic}></sui-codeblock>
  <p>
    That <code>my-card</code> element is now styled — drop a
    <code>&lt;my-card&gt;</code> in any page and the styles ship with
    it. No external CSS, no build step, no class-name collisions.
  </p>

  <h2>Scope</h2>
  <p>
    Inline styles are scoped per-instance. The <code>:host</code>
    pseudo-element targets the component's own element; any descendant
    selector targets the shadow tree. Outer-page styles do not bleed
    in, and the component does not leak out.
  </p>
  <sui-codeblock code={code.scoping}></sui-codeblock>

  <h2>Tokens, not literals</h2>
  <p>
    Read tokens from the document root. A literal value — a hex color, a
    pixel size, a fixed font-family — defeats theming, breaks dark mode,
    and locks the component to one brand voice. Use
    <code>var(--token)</code> with no fallback; tokens.css is loaded
    before any component renders, so the resolver always wins.
  </p>
  <sui-codeblock code={code.tokens}></sui-codeblock>
  <p>
    The four layers — semantic surfaces, tone ramps, brand palette,
    primitives — keep the choice of token aligned with intent. A card
    reads <code>var(--surface-1)</code>; a status alert reads
    <code>var(--tone-warning-bg)</code>; a headline reads
    <code>var(--font-display)</code>. Reach for a primitive only when
    no semantic token captures the role.
  </p>

  <h2>Status tones, not palette colors</h2>
  <p>
    Tone ramps are status surfaces, not brand colors. <code>success</code>
    is the right token for a positive confirmation; <code>primary</code> is
    a brand action. Mixing the two breaks every theme that ships its
    own success color.
  </p>
  <sui-codeblock code={code.tone}></sui-codeblock>

  <h2>Layout primitives</h2>
  <p>
    Reach for <code>&lt;sui-stack&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;sui-cluster&gt;</code>,
    and <code>&lt;sui-grid&gt;</code> before reaching for a hand-rolled
    flex layout. They encode the design system's spacing scale, the
    breakpoints, and the responsive defaults — so a layout that reads
    <code>gap="3"</code> in one place looks the same wherever it's reused.
  </p>
  <sui-codeblock code={code.layout}></sui-codeblock>
  <p>
    For one-off layouts where a layout primitive doesn't fit, an inline
    <code>display: flex; gap: var(--space-3)</code> is the right call —
    not because flex is forbidden, but because reaching for the layout
    primitive first means most components stay consistent by default.
  </p>

  <div class="page-nav">
    <a class="btn" href="/theming">← Theming</a>
    <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/composition">Composition →</a>
  </div>
</div>
